  "entries": {
    "grub": {
      "word": "GRUB",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To dig in or under the ground, generally for an object that is difficult to reach or extricate; to be occupied in digging.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To drudge; to do menial work. Richardson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To dig; to dig up by the roots; to root out by digging; -- followed by up; as, to grub up trees, rushes, or sedge. They do not attempt to grub up the root of sin. Hare.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To supply with food. [Slang] Dickens.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Zoöl.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The larva of an insect, especially of a beetle; -- called also grubworm. See Illust. of Goldsmith beetle, under Goldsmith. Yet your butterfly was a grub. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A short, thick man; a dwarf. [Obs.] Carew.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Victuals; food. [Slang] Halliwell. Grub ax or axe, a kind of mattock used in grubbing up roots, etc. roots, etc. described by Dr. Johnson as \"much inhabited by writers of small histories, dictionaries, and temporary poems, whence any mean production is called grubstreet.\" As an adjective, suitable to, or resembling the production of, Grub Street. I 'd sooner ballads write, and grubstreet lays. Gap.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
